      <span style="background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.12); color: #f59e0b; border: 1px solid rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.35); padding: 4px 14px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 14px;">HANDOVER INSPECTION</span>
      <h2 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.35; margin-bottom: 18px;">Destination Handover, Odometer Sign-Off &amp; Joint Condition Inspection for Bike Transportation In Shiggaon</h2>
      <div style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #cbd5e1;"><p>When a two‑wheeler or motorcycle arrives at its destination, the delivery handover follows a strict protocol to guarantee that the vehicle’s condition, mileage, and ownership documentation are all verified before the customer signs off. The process is designed to protect both the carrier and the recipient and to prevent disputes after delivery.</p>

<p>The handover begins with a daylight re‑inspection of the vehicle. The delivery driver or a designated inspector compares the current exterior and interior condition with the photographs taken at pickup. Any scratches, dents, or fluid leaks that were not present at the time of dispatch are noted.</p>
<p>This visual check is performed under natural light to avoid shadows or glare that could hide defects. If a discrepancy is found, the inspector records the issue in the delivery log and photographs the new damage for the carrier’s records.</p>

<p>Next, the odometer is read and verified against the mileage listed on the LR bilty (Logistics Receipt). The driver records the current mileage on a printed or digital form, then cross‑checks it with the bilty’s figure.</p>
<p>A difference of more than five miles triggers an audit, and the driver must report the variance immediately to the logistics manager. If the odometer reading matches the bilty, the driver marks the mileage as verified and moves to the final step.</p>

<p>The signed condition sign‑off is the contractual conclusion of the handover. The recipient receives a copy of the delivery report, which includes the daylight inspection notes, odometer confirmation, and any discrepancy logs.</p>
<p>Both parties sign the report, acknowledging that the vehicle was delivered in the agreed condition and mileage. The signed document is then scanned into the carrier’s system for record‑keeping, and a copy is returned to the recipient for their records.</p>

